  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Knowledge about spatial distribution and clustering of urban settlements is necessary in planning urban environments, designing infrastructure, preparing for emergencies and even service provision in a spatially equitable manner. In this study we sought to investigate the spatial distribution and clustering of urban settlements across Ghana. We utilized the high resolution GRID3 settlement data and a set of Geographic Information System (GIS)-based spatial statistical methods to analyze distribution and clustering of urban settlements in Ghana. Settlement points were subjected to kernel density estimation (KDE) for identification of urban settlement hotspots, nearest neighbor distance (NND) analysis in order to study proximity of settlements and Ripley’s K and L functions in order to analyze clustering across different spatial scales. A clearly diverse spatial distribution pattern was observed and the highest settlement density was found in the Greater Accra, Ashanti, Central, Eastern and Western regions and the settlement was relatively dispersed throughout the northern regions. Major urban centers and settlement hotspots identified by KDE were located in Southern Ghana while NND analysis revealed significantly reduced inter settlement distances at all scales in Southern Ghana as compared to the northern savannah regions indicating highly aggregated/compact settlement patterns in the South and dispersed settlement patterns at all scale in the North while K and L functions confirmed that settlement patterns were statistically significantly aggregated across multiple spatial scales. These results provide strong empirical evidence to inform urban planning, infrastructure development planning, emergency preparedness, access to health services and sustainable spatial planning policy. These findings also highlight the usefulness of high-resolution settlement data such as that provided by GRID3 and advanced spatial statistics in national-level settlement analysis and evidence-based policy development in Ghana.",
